  "formula": "proof = Sp * As            (proof load of the screw; Sp psi, As in^2, result in lbf)\nstrip = pi * d * (le*d) * tau * 0.5      (thread strip in the tapped material; le*d = engagement length)\n  nut on the back: strip = 1.05 * proof;  heat-set insert: strip = table value in `data.insertPullout` (0 = no data)\npullLimit = min(proof, strip)             (0 if strip is 0)\nslipLimit = PRELOAD * Sp * As * MU        (sideways: friction of the clamped joint), PRELOAD = 0.70, MU = 0.15\nstraight pull: safe = pullLimit / duty;  sideways: safe = min(slipLimit, pullLimit) / duty\n(inch page: forces are already in pounds-force, reported as lb)\nsmallestSize = first size, in ascending order, with safe >= load. governing = 'the hole' if strip < proof, 'the screw' if not, 'no data' if strip = 0.",
  "assumptions": [
   "Rough sizing only; the page says not to use it for anything that could hurt someone if it let go (vehicle parts, climbing gear, ceiling mounts over people, pressure equipment).",
   "Preload is 70% of proof; sideways load is carried by friction at mu = 0.15 on one face only.",
   "Thread strip is a simplified shear-area estimate; heat-set insert pull-out is vendor dependent, order of magnitude only.",
   "Safety factor is applied once, to the load; with more than two screws the load may not share evenly."
  ],
